{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>All the commandment which I command you this day.</b> He attached 'all the commandment' to the matter of idolatry that he mentioned. For it is <i>ḥerem</i> (a ban), to teach you that whoever abominates idolatry is as one who fulfills all the commandments. And the Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: whoever denies idolatry is as one who affirms the entire Torah, and whoever affirms idolatry is as one who denies the entire Torah." "<b>And in the Midrash</b> of Eileh HaDevarim Rabbah: 'All the commandment' — entirely a commandment, until you complete the entire commandment. The explanation of this is that if one began a commandment, one should finish it. And they further expounded: 'All the commandment which I command you this day' — if you have begun a commandment, finish it, for it is called only after the one who completes it. As it is said (Joshua 24:32): 'And the bones of Joseph, which the children of Israel brought up out of Egypt, they buried in Shechem' — but was it not Moses alone who occupied himself with them, as it is said (Exodus 13:19): 'And Moses took the bones of Joseph with him'? Rather, since he did not manage to complete the burial and the children of Israel completed it, it is called after them."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:37.304082614Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}